---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/compare/yotpo-email-sms-vs-postscript";
const title = "Yotpo Email & SMS to Postscript: Shopify migration decisions";
const desc = "Assess the Postscript migration route after Yotpo messaging retirement, with destination-specific acceptance checks.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can I keep sending through Yotpo during this migration?",
    "answer": "Do not plan on it. The messaging login reports decommissioned dashboard and background services. Confirm available records with support; do not promise a retired-sender rollback."
  },
  {
    "question": "What is the key Postscript acceptance decision?",
    "answer": "Verify reviewed SMS subscriber transfer, capture and reply ownership, with a separate explicit email owner."
  },
  {
    "question": "Do Reviews and Loyalty move with messaging?",
    "answer": "Treat them as separate services and data dependencies. Name the owner of review requests and points messages, and verify any required destination integration."
  },
  {
    "question": "Were the migration checks on this page performed?",
    "answer": "No. They are proposed checks for a receiving account. Production delivery, permissions, recovery and account-specific costs require separate validation."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <nav><a class="link" href="/apps">Tool catalog</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">Selection framework</a></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Yotpo Email &amp; SMS is a migration question now. Its messaging login reports that the application and background services are decommissioned. Reviews and Loyalty are separate decisions. An old export or implementation guide does not prove that a retired dashboard can still be opened. For Postscript, scope the SMS transfer separately from the email program and decide who will own replies and new subscriber capture.</p></header>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Transfer an eligible SMS list, not every phone number</h2>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13563834-migrating-from-yotpo-smsbump-to-postscript">Postscript migration guide</a> describes a subscriber upload form and compliance review. It asks merchants to retain opt-in form evidence. This is a reviewed list transfer, not a promise of automatic profile, email or journey migration.</p>
      <p>Prepare your saved subscriber CSV with source, export date and supporting disclosures. Exclude unsubscribed records from the active import while retaining suppression evidence separately. If the only file contains phone numbers without channel status, stop the promotional import and resolve the gap with the receiving team.</p>
      <h2>Assign an email owner before the SMS cutover</h2>
      <p>This guide covers the documented Postscript SMS migration route. It does not establish which email features your destination contract includes. Inventory newsletters, order education, account messages and review invitations, then assign each to an email system or a separately verified service. Do not leave them ownerless because the phone list arrived successfully.</p>
      <p>Proposed acceptance case: a customer has email permission but no SMS permission and abandons checkout. Verify that the email owner can evaluate its own recovery eligibility while Postscript sends no promotional text. Repeat with an SMS-only customer and a completed purchase to inspect channel scope and recovery exit behavior.</p>
      <h2>Reconnect acquisition and reply handling</h2>
      <p>Postscript’s historical sequence disables old Yotpo growth tools before export and activates receiving tools after migration. With decommissioned services, inspect your live storefront and checkout for leftover embeds and dead signup destinations instead of relying on retired settings.</p>
      <p>Use a staff-controlled number to check the current disclosure, submission result and subscription record through each capture surface. Assign an operator for inbound questions and opt-out exceptions. Test a stock question and an opt-out request separately; a support reply must not accidentally renew promotional permission.</p>
      <h2>Keep review requests and sender continuity explicit</h2>
      <p>Keeping Yotpo Reviews or Loyalty does not restore Yotpo messaging. Name the system responsible for each review invitation and points reminder. Require a real destination event sample before rebuilding a review-dependent text; do not substitute an ordinary purchase event for a review submission.</p>
      <p>Preserve the old sender identity and number documentation where available, but ask Postscript about transfer eligibility, registration and readiness for your account. A completed subscriber upload is not proof that the production number can send. Schedule activation only after the receiving team confirms the required setup.</p>
      <h2>Release checklist, costs and evidence limits</h2>
      <p>Keep destination sends paused until eligible counts, suppressions, capture routes and representative queues reconcile. Name one owner for every automated message across Shopify, retained Yotpo modules and the receiving stack. If a destination check fails, pause that destination queue and repair it; the retired Yotpo sender is not a rollback option.</p>
      <p>Compare the receiving subscription, messaging usage, implementation work and retained Reviews or Loyalty costs. Sequenzy or another email service belongs in that budget only if its required integration and operating role are demonstrated. There is no evidenced universal winner or fixed price here.</p>
      <p>Checked against primary supplier material on 24 November 2026 and the <a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">Yotpo decommission notice</a>. These are proposed acceptance cases, not performed merchant tests. Historical data recovery, legal sufficiency, account entitlements and production delivery remain unverified. Related migration assessments: <a class="link" href="/compare/yotpo-email-sms-vs-omnisend">Omnisend</a>, <a class="link" href="/compare/yotpo-email-sms-vs-postscript">Postscript</a>, <a class="link" href="/compare/yotpo-email-sms-vs-privy">Privy</a>.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
